{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,9]],"date-time":"2026-10-09T12:48:56Z","timestamp":1791550136324,"version":"4.3.4"},"reference-count":48,"publisher":"Wiley","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1791244800000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1791244800000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["J. Plant Nutr. Soil Sci."],"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Background<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>On ca. 10% of the forested sites in the Bavarian Alps (Germany) mountain, forest soils (so\u2010called folic histosols) are characterized by thick organic layers overlying poorly weathered calcareous parent material.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Aim<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>These organic layers play a critical role in releasing water and nutrients for vegetation, yet they are also sensitive to climate change, with warming\u2010induced increases in respiration potentially reducing their thickness and water\u2010holding capacity.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>We used the process\u2010based water balance model LWF\u2010Brook90 to simulate soil water availability for 117 sites represented by georeferenced folic histosol profiles and to study the effect of different climate change scenarios (Representative Concentration Pathway [RCP] 2.6, 4.5, and 8.5) and humus loss (0%\u201375%) on the water balance. Specifically, we used high\u2010resolution climate data and HYPROP\u2010based Mualem\u2013van Genuchten parameters to study potential changes in soil water retention and drought risk.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Simulating the effects of changes in precipitation and temperature in combination with organic layer degradation and the resultant loss of water\u2010holding capacity led to significant increases in predicted transpiration deficits. A 50% reduction in organic layer thickness resulted in a distinct decrease of plant\u2010available moisture even under the optimistic RCP 2.6 scenario. Under severe climate change (scenario RCP 8.5) and 75% organic layer loss, water availability shifted up to two levels on a seven\u2010part relative scale of moisture regime. Although such extreme levels (&gt;50%) of organic layer loss are unlikely under closed canopy conditions, they may occur following stand\u2010replacing disturbance events such as large\u2010scale windthrows or bark\u2010beetle outbreaks, which are expected to increase under climate change. Drought\u2010induced reductions of vegetation productivity may inhibit litter production and the replenishment of organic layers.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusion<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Our findings highlight the vulnerability of sites dominated by thick organic layers to climate\u2010driven water stress. By the end of the century, even under an optimistic scenario (no humus loss, RCP 4.5), the site water balance class on folic histosols is projected to decline by one class for the drier sites (75th percentile). Preventing a downward spiral of mountain forests triggered by climate change requires a holistic silvicultural concept for preserving folic histosols.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","DOI":"10.1002\/jpln.70129","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]],"date-time":"2026-10-06T12:26:55Z","timestamp":1791289615000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Climate Change and Reduction of Soil Organic Layer Thickness Increases Drought Risk in Mountain Forests of the Northern Alps"],"prefix":"10.1002","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-1942-3980","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marc","family":"K\u00fchnbach","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bavarian State Institute of Forestry (LWF)  Freising Germany"},{"name":"TUM School of Life Sciences Chair of Ecosystem Dynamics and Forest Management Technical University of Munich  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Axel","family":"Wellpott","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bavarian State Institute of Forestry (LWF)  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3338-3402","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rupert","family":"Seidl","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"TUM School of Life Sciences Chair of Ecosystem Dynamics and Forest Management Technical University of Munich  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hans\u2010Joachim","family":"Klemmt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bavarian State Institute of Forestry (LWF)  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Klaas","family":"Wellhausen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Bavarian State Institute of Forestry (LWF)  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2758-9324","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"J\u00f6rg","family":"Ewald","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Weihenstephan\u2014Triesdorf University of Applied Sciences (HSWT)  Freising Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,6]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_9_2_1","volume-title":"Bodenkundliche Kartieranleitung","author":"Ad\u2010hoc Working Group on Soil","year":"2005"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_3_1","unstructured":"Bochter R. W.Neuerburg andW.Zech.1981.Humus und Humusschwund im Gebirge. Berchtesgaden: Nationalparkverwaltung No. 2."},{"key":"e_1_2_9_4_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/gcb.17121"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1051\/forest:2006042"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_6_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s13595\u2010018\u20100788\u20105"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/s0012\u20108252(00)00011\u20108"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_8_1","first-page":"1","volume-title":"Zeigerwerte von Pflanzen in Mitteleuropa","author":"Ellenberg H.","year":"2001"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_9_1","first-page":"45","article-title":"Waldinformationssystem Nordalpen\u2014WINALP sammelt Wissen zum Schutz der Bergw\u00e4lder","volume":"30","author":"Ewald J.","year":"2009","journal-title":"Waldforschung Aktuell"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_10_1","first-page":"25","article-title":"Werkzeuge zur Bestimmung der Waldtypen im bayerischen Hochgebirge","volume":"5","author":"Ewald J.","year":"2007","journal-title":"Wald\u00f6kologie Online"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_11_1","volume-title":"Forstliche Forschungsberichte M\u00fcnchen No. 220","author":"Ewald J.","year":"2020"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_12_1","first-page":"79","volume-title":"Informationstechnologie f\u00fcr Eine Nachhaltige Landbewirtschaftung\u2014Fokus Forstwirtschaft","author":"Ewald J.","year":"2012"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_13_1","volume-title":"The Forest in Germany: Selected Results of the Fourth National Forest Inventory","author":"Federal Ministry of Food and Agriculture","year"